{"name":"Green Pepper","hex":"#97bc62","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green pepper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The green, unripe, form of the sweet bell pepper.","A green variety of dried peppercorn, made from the unripe berries by treating them in a manner that retains the green color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green_pepper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Capsicum annuum","blocks":[{"text":"Capsicum annuum is a flowering plant in the family Solanaceae (nightshades), native to the northern regions of South America and to southwestern North America. The plant produces berries of many colors including red, green, and yellow, often with pungent taste. It is one of the oldest cultivated crops, with domestication dating back to around 6,000 years ago in regions of Mexico. The genus Capsicum has over 30 species but Capsicum annuum is the primary species in its genus, as it has been widely cultivated for human consumption for a substantial amount of time and has spread across the world. This species has many uses in culinary and ornamental applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Capsicum_annuum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-027.jpg/330px-Capsicum_annuum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capsicum_annuum","wikidata":"Q1380"},{"title":"Chili pepper","blocks":[{"text":"Chili peppers, also spelled chile or chilli, are varieties of berry-fruit plants from the genus Capsicum, which are members of the nightshade family Solanaceae, cultivated for their pungency. They are used as a spice to add pungency in many cuisines. Capsaicin and the related capsaicinoids give chili peppers their intensity when ingested or applied topically. Chili peppers exhibit a range of heat and flavors. This diversity is the reason behind the availability of different types of chili powder, each offering its own taste and heat level."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg/330px-Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chili_pepper","wikidata":"Q165199"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Dundicut_Pepper_in_Behbahan%2C_Iran_III.jpg/500px-Dundicut_Pepper_in_Behbahan%2C_Iran_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Dundicut_Pepper_in_Behbahan%2C_Iran_III.jpg/1280px-Dundicut_Pepper_in_Behbahan%2C_Iran_III.jpg?utm_source